# Keep shared spec data and fixtures as LF on every platform so the
# data-driven test suite parses identically on Windows checkouts.
*.tsv text eol=lf
test/spec/files/** text eol=lf
# The embedded copy of test/spec/signature.tsv (docs/design/
# SIGNATURES.0.md): byte-compared with it by go/sig_test.go, so it
# keeps the same line endings on every platform.
go/sigdecl.txt text eol=lf
# The embedded teaching pack (G11 phase 1): copies of docs/skill/*.md
# and grammar/aontu.abnf staged into the Go command's own package,
# because //go:embed cannot read above its own directory. Both suites
# byte-compare them with their sources, so they keep the same line
# endings on every platform. The globs above already cover .md, .tsv
# and .abnf; this is here so the directory is not mistaken for
# hand-written content. Regenerate with `make helpdoc`, never by hand.
go/cmd/aontu/helpdoc/** text eol=lf
# The starting documents `aontu init` writes (G11 phase 6). Real files,
# run by the suites where they live, and staged into both ports by the
# same generator -- so they are byte-compared like the pack above. The
# globs below do not reach them: two are `.aontu` and one is a shell
# script, and a scaffold that arrived with CRLF would be written back
# out that way by a command whose two ports must write the same bytes.
docs/skill/init/** text eol=lf
# The built-in aontu: models (ADR-036). aontu/ is the source and
# go/aontumodel/ its committed mirror, because //go:embed cannot read
# above its own directory. THE BYTES ARE EMBEDDED VERBATIM, so a CRLF
# checkout would put CRLF in the binary, and `aontu fmt` -- which
# normalises line endings -- could never round-trip its own bundled
# models. The raw string literals these replaced hid that: Go discards
# \r from a raw string, and //go:embed keeps every byte. The `.aontu`
# globs at the end of this file set highlighting, not line endings.
# Regenerate with `make aontu`, never by hand.
aontu/** text eol=lf
go/aontumodel/** text eol=lf

# The same rule, for the same reason, over every other file the suite
# READS AS DATA rather than compiles: the documentation is executed
# (ts/test/docs.test.ts extracts fenced examples and runs them), and the
# published grammars are parsed to check their rule and builtin sets
# against the engine (ts/test/grammar.test.ts). Both broke on a CRLF
# checkout, and both broke QUIETLY -- the example extractor matched zero
# blocks and reported a documentation file with nothing in it, and the
# grammar's rule slice ran past a terminator it could no longer find,
# swallowing every later rule.
#
# docs/skill/ is covered here as well, though its reader happened to
# survive: examples.md fences carry no language tag, so the strip that
# would have been line-ending sensitive is a no-op there. Covered
# because that is a property of how the file is written today, not a
# property anyone maintains.
#
# This is belt to the readers' braces, not a substitute for them: the
# extractors also normalise line endings themselves, because a file can
# reach them from a source tarball or an editor rather than a checkout,
# and a test that only works under one git configuration is a test that
# does not work.
*.md text eol=lf
*.abnf text eol=lf
*.gbnf text eol=lf
*.lark text eol=lf
*.tmLanguage.json text eol=lf

# GITHUB'S HIGHLIGHTING, UNTIL LINGUIST HAS THE LANGUAGE.
#
# github.com colours a blob only for a language Linguist knows, and
# Linguist takes a new one at 2000 files per extension indexed in a
# year, spread across unrepeated repositories. `.aontu` is nowhere near
# that and will not be until the language is used outside this
# organisation, so the 415 sources here render as plain text.
#
# `linguist-language` is the lever that does not wait: it is documented
# as setting HIGHLIGHTING as well as classification, and YAML is the
# closest grammar already in Linguist -- `#` comments, `key: value`,
# quoted strings and numbers all land. It reads two spellings wrong,
# the `&:` spread (YAML sees an anchor) and the `@"..."` include, which
# is the price of the other ninety per cent.
#
# `-linguist-detectable` keeps the repository's language bar honest
# while that alias stands: colour the files, do not claim the project
# is written in YAML.
#
# This is a stopgap with an end condition. When grammar/aontu.tmLanguage.json
# is accepted into Linguist, both lines come out in the same commit --
# they would then OVERRIDE the real grammar with a worse one.
*.aontu   linguist-language=YAML -linguist-detectable
*.aontu linguist-language=YAML -linguist-detectable
